course: dictionary
lesson: 16119
title: Where is it? Understanding whereabouts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word whereabouts is built from two small words, where and about. It is used to talk about a place, usually when you want to know where someone or something is. Context helps you spot it: look for words like missing, lost or find.
explain: As a noun, whereabouts means the place where someone or something is. «The police asked the public for help finding the missing girl's whereabouts.» The clues are missing and finding, so whereabouts means the place where she is.
explain: As an adverb in a question, whereabouts asks in or near what place. «Whereabouts do you live?» means "in or near what place do you live?" People often say it in a friendly, informal way, especially in the UK, Ireland and Australia. «Whereabouts in town?» asks for a rough place, not an exact address.
explain: Whereabouts is also an important safety word. If you are out, always tell an adult your whereabouts, so they know where you are. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: "always tell an adult the place where you are" still makes sense.
fact: Whereabouts means the place where someone or something is.
fact: As a question, «Whereabouts do you live?» asks in or near what place.
fact: Whereabouts can take a singular or a plural verb: «His whereabouts is unknown» and «Their whereabouts are unknown» are both correct.
fact: In everyday talk, «whereabouts» is more informal than «where», as in «Whereabouts in town?»
fact: Always tell an adult your whereabouts when you are out, so they can find you if you need help.
exercise: «The police asked for help finding the missing man's whereabouts.» What does whereabouts mean here? | The clothes he was wearing | *The place where he is | His favourite food | The name of his dog
exercise: «Whereabouts do you live?» What is this question asking? | Who your best friends are | What time you go to bed | *In or near what place you live | How old you are
exercise: Which should you always tell an adult when you are out? | Your best joke | Your shoe size | Your favourite colour | *Your whereabouts
test: Mia asks, «Whereabouts in town is the swimming pool?» What is she asking? | The time the pool opens | The name of the lifeguard | *A rough place in town where the pool is | The price of a swim
test: Dad says, «Tell me your whereabouts when you are walking home.» What does he want to know? | How fast you can run | The way you walk | *The place where you are | The shoes you are wearing
exam: «The whereabouts of the lost dog are still unknown.» Which meaning fits whereabouts best? | The sound the dog makes | *The place where the dog is, said in a question | The place where the dog is | The food the dog likes
